Here's the same code with added comments for clarity:

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.BufferedWriter;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.FileWriter;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Define the number of lines to read from the file and write to.
        int numLines = 1000;

        // Loop through each line in the range of numbers.
        for (int i = 1; i <= numLines; i++) {
            String s = "This is line " + i + ".\n";

            try {
                // Open a BufferedReader to read from the file.
                BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("test.txt"));

                // Open a BufferedWriter to write to the file, appending to the end if it exists.
                BufferedWriter bw = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter("test.txt", true));

                int j;
                // Read each character from the file into the s string.
                while ((j = br.read()) != -1) {
                    // Write the substring of length 0 or less (the current line) to the writer.
                    bw.write(s.substring(0, Math.min(j, s.length())));
                }

                // Close the reader and writer when done.
                br.close();
                bw.close();

            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Print any errors that occur during file operations.
                System.out.println(e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code reads lines from a file called "test.txt" in reverse order, adding each line to the end of the previous line. The `true` argument in the `FileWriter` constructor appends to the file instead of overwriting it. If an error occurs during this process (e.g., because the file does not exist), the code prints an error message and continues running.
